this year three different teams used this nice and simple training idea: give the model a hint it won’t have later, let it solve the task with the hint, and then teach it to give the same answer without one (like a student working through the solutions manual, then closing it)

@jonashuebotter et al. (SDPO) used error messages and feedback as the hint: on a coding benchmark, a Qwen3-8B trained this way scored 48.8% vs 41.2% with standard RL, and caught up to standard RL’s final score with 4x less sampling
https://t.co/9wybQMK93e

@cursor_ai used it to train Composer 2.5: when the model makes a mistake mid-task, they insert a short hint right at that point. their post also lists what the model learned to cheat with, from digging through a leftover Python cache to decompiling Java code
https://t.co/lbSXZF7kIA

@perplexity_ai trained on real failures from production: in a live test with real users, tool errors dropped from 3.56% to 2.38% and the signs of unhappy users from 4.12% to 3.41% (they also published the test that showed no effect, which I respect a lot!)
https://t.co/gBS51MXXRU

then last month came the reality check: Zhong et al. ran 10 of these self-distillation methods against plain RL (GRPO) on 4 agent benchmarks with three Qwen3.5 sizes, 132 training runs and over 12,000 GPU-hours. only 2 of the 10 beat plain RL on average, the best one won in 6 of 12 settings, and spending more compute didn’t reliably help
https://t.co/TwmThsfera

so the idea is good, and how much it helps depends a lot on the task: the hint is where your knowledge about the task goes in, and that part seems to matter more than the method around it (sigh)

